---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout basePath="" title="Relationship Dynamics | Waitlist">
  <!-- PAGE HEADER -->
  <section class="pt-32 pb-16 px-4 bg-brand-navy text-center border-b border-brand-navy-light">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded-full bg-white/10 border border-white/20 text-brand-saffron text-xs sm:text-sm font-semibold tracking-wide uppercase mb-6">
        <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-brand-saffron animate-pulse"></span>
        In Development
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-extrabold text-white mb-6 tracking-tight">Why do your relationships keep failing?</h1>
      <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-slate-300 leading-relaxed font-medium max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        You aren't broken. You're just running an incompatible algorithm.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE PROBLEM -->
  <section class="py-24 px-4 bg-white">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <div class="text-center mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-6">The Collision of Patterns</h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-brand-text leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          Love is not enough to sustain a relationship. When the honeymoon phase ends, your underlying behavioral patterns take over. If your pattern requires constant validation, and your partner's pattern defaults to emotional distance under stress, you are not experiencing a "communication issue." You are experiencing a systemic collision.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-16">
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-brand-border p-8 rounded-2xl">
          <div class="text-3xl mb-4">💔</div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-brand-navy text-xl mb-3">The "Fixer" Trap</h3>
          <p class="text-brand-text text-sm leading-relaxed">
            You constantly attract partners who need saving. You pour all your energy into fixing them, until you are completely drained. When they finally stabilize, they leave, or you resent them. You think you're just a "giver," but you're running a pattern that equates love with utility.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-brand-border p-8 rounded-2xl">
          <div class="text-3xl mb-4">🛡️</div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-brand-navy text-xl mb-3">The Saboteur</h3>
          <p class="text-brand-text text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Everything is going perfectly. They are kind, stable, and loving. Suddenly, you feel bored. Or you pick a fight over nothing. Or you convince yourself they are lying. You aren't falling out of love; your pattern is violently rejecting stability because chaos feels like "home."
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-brand-light-bg border border-brand-saffron/30 p-10 rounded-3xl text-center">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-brand-navy mb-4">The Relationship Dynamics System</h2>
        <p class="text-brand-text mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto">
          We are currently developing a system to map two conflicting behavioral blueprints against each other. It will diagnose exactly why you clash, where your blind spots overlap, and how to stop the toxic loops before they destroy the connection.
        </p>
        
        <div class="max-w-md mx-auto bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-brand-border shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-brand-navy mb-2">Join the Waitlist</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-brand-text-muted mb-4">Be the first to access the relationship diagnosis tool when it launches.</p>
          <form class="flex flex-col gap-3" onsubmit="event.preventDefault(); alert('You have been added to the waitlist!');">
            <input type="email" placeholder="Enter your email address" class="w-full p-3 border border-brand-border rounded-lg outline-none focus:border-brand-saffron" required>
            <button type="submit" class="w-full py-3 bg-brand-navy text-white rounded-lg font-bold hover:bg-brand-saffron transition-colors">Notify Me</button>
          </form>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
